/E (Modern applications are updated frequently to introduce features, resolve defects, and address security issues. A release process that depends entirely on manual commands may vary between operators or environments. A forgotten step, incorrect configuration, or wrong artifact can cause downtime or make a release difficult to reproduce. As the frequency of releases increases, teams need a controlled and repeatable delivery process. DevOps combines people, processes, and automation to improve collaboration between development and operations. Continuous integration encourages frequent validation of changes, while continuous delivery makes verified software available for controlled release. Containers package the application and its dependencies. Kubernetes manages container workloads and helps maintain the desired application state. Cloud services provide scalable infrastructure and managed capabilities.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 162 0 R
/T (1.2 Problem Statement)
/E (1.2 Problem Statement)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (The problem addressed by this project is the lack of a standardised, auditable, and observable application deployment workflow. Manual deployment can result in inconsistent builds, unclear version history, late discovery of defects, and limited evidence about why a release failed. A suitable system should automate repeatable tasks, stop when quality checks fail, deploy a known image, verify service health, and provide a documented recovery method.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
