375 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Kubernetes manages containerised workloads through declarative resources. A Deployment defines a desired number of replicas and controls rolling updates. Pods run the containers, while a Service provides a stable network endpoint. Readiness probes indicate whether a container can receive traffic; liveness probes can restart containers that are stuck or unhealthy. Resource requests and limits help control scheduling and consumption.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 167 0 R
/T (2.7 Amazon Web Services)
/E (2.7 Amazon Web Services)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (AWS provides infrastructure and managed services for hosting applications. Amazon EKS provides a managed Kubernetes control plane, and Amazon ECR stores container images. IAM controls identities and permissions. VPCs, subnets, security groups, routing, and load balancers govern network connectivity. Although managed services reduce some operational tasks, users remain responsible for workload configuration, access controls, cost monitoring, and many reliability decisions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 167 0 R
/T (2.8 Prometheus and Grafana)
/E (2.8 Prometheus and Grafana)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Prometheus collects time-series metrics from configured endpoints and supports queries and alert rules. Grafana visualises metrics in dashboards. Useful measurements include request rate, error rate, response latency, CPU and memory consumption, Pod restarts, and replica availability. Logs provide event-level details that help explain a metric anomaly. Monitoring should be designed around actionable questions rather than collecting every possible metric.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
