511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Docker provides a consistent application package that includes required runtime components. It reduces dependency differences between environments and supports repeatable testing. The image should contain only necessary files, use maintained base images, and avoid embedded credentials. Multi-stage builds can reduce the final image size when the application requires a compilation step.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 174 0 R
/T (3.17 Why Kubernetes?)
/E (3.17 Why Kubernetes?)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
513 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Kubernetes is useful when an application benefits from declarative deployment, replica management, service discovery, and rolling updates. It may be excessive for a very small service that could run on a simpler managed platform. The project chooses Kubernetes because orchestration and rollout behaviour are core learning objectives.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
514 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 174 0 R
/T (3.18 Why EKS and ECR?)
/E (3.18 Why EKS and ECR?)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
515 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (EKS provides a managed Kubernetes control plane and integrates with AWS identity and networking. ECR stores images and integrates with AWS permissions. These services reduce some management tasks but do not eliminate the need for workload security, capacity planning, monitoring, and cost control.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
516 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 174 0 R
/T (3.19 Design Trade-offs)
/E (3.19 Design Trade-offs)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
517 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Managed Kubernetes improves consistency but can have a higher baseline cost than a local cluster. A self-hosted CI server offers flexibility but requires patching and maintenance. Rich monitoring increases visibility but adds resource and configuration overhead. The design should select the smallest platform that meets the project goals and document why each component is needed.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [11]
>>
